    rationale:
      "Picked up as a scheduled routine (\"continue toward a profitable, growing business\") with an empty free NEXT_STEPS backlog (confirmed via direct grep: the same five status=\"todo\" operator/legal/infra-gated entries). Fold-scanned the remote first: `git branch -r --no-merged origin/main` returned ~430 branches, but spot-checking the most-recently-pushed ones (e.g. claude/upbeat-dijkstra-6nb9f0, the source of mark-lead-paid-live-stage-regression-guard) showed their diffs already fully present on main under different (squash-merged) commit SHAs — git's ancestor check doesn't recognize squash merges, so this repo's `--no-merged` output is dominated by that noise rather than genuine stuck work; no live fold candidate found. mcp__github__list_pull_requests confirmed the one open PR (#202) is still the same weeks-stale unrelated draft prior iterations have noted.\n\nDispatched a research agent primed with the full AGENT_PROTOCOL text and the tier 1-4 empty-backlog fallback order, told to independently verify any candidate and confirm it wasn't already covered. It found that resolvePreviewOpenedAt only bounds body.openedAt against the future (MAX_PREVIEW_OPENED_AT_FUTURE_SKEW_MS); an arbitrarily old past value is accepted verbatim, persisted as Lead.lastPreviewOpenAt, and route.ts's debounce check derived its \"now\" from that same client-resolved value (`Date.parse(openedAt)`) rather than real server time.\n\nIndependently traced the actual exploit before implementing, because the prior iteration (preview-open-openedat-future-skew-guard) had explicitly considered and rejected a past-side bound, reasoning: \"an old spoofed date at most looks stale immediately and gets naturally overwritten by the next genuine open... a materially different, much lower-severity gaming vector.\" That reasoning holds for a single stray spoof followed by a real visitor, but does not hold against a caller acting in its own interest: POST /api/preview/[slug]/open is public and unauthenticated, keyed only by a leakable lead slug, and its own doc comment states the debounce \"already limits legitimate writes per lead\" while the per-IP rate limit \"only guards against a scripted burst.\" Once lastPreviewOpenAt is pinned to any old past value (via one call, since shouldPersistPreviewOpen always allows the very first write for a lead), real \"now\" minus that ancient checkpoint trivially clears PREVIEW_OPEN_DEBOUNCE_MS (30 minutes) on every subsequent call, however soon after and however many — the debounce does not recover on its own, it stays permanently defeated for that lead until a legitimate recent write happens to land, which an attacker controls the timing of by simply not sending one. This is a genuine write-amplification / anti-abuse bypass (unlimited real recordLeadPreviewOpen writes bounded only by the per-IP rate limit, trivially multiplied across IPs), not merely a data-quality nit — it defeats a guard the code's own comments describe as load-bearing.\n\nReproduced end to end against the pre-fix code on a real local.db + dev server before touching anything: POSTed `{\"openedAt\":\"2001-01-01T00:00:00.000Z\"}` to /api/preview/serene-salon/open (outreach_sent-stage demo lead) and confirmed the row's lastPreviewOpenAt was written as the literal 2001 value (not clamped, since resolvePreviewOpenedAt had no past bound); confirmed an immediate real follow-up call (same instant, no spoofed body) was NOT debounced — recorded:true — because the buggy debounce comparison read as \"29 years have passed\" against the persisted 2001 checkpoint. Also confirmed grepping prompt_process.tsx for \"openedAt\"/\"preview-open\" surfaced no other entry addressing the past direction — the five prior hits were exclusively the future-skew guard and the unrelated *-field-length-cap series.\n\nFixed with two changes, both minimal and same-contract as the existing future-skew guard: (1) added MAX_PREVIEW_OPENED_AT_PAST_SKEW_MS (5 minutes) and a second guard clause in resolvePreviewOpenedAt, symmetric to the existing future check — this is the actual fix for the exploit, since it stops the persisted checkpoint from ever being pinned more than 5 minutes from real time in either direction, restoring the invariant that real-now-minus-checkpoint only exceeds the 30-minute debounce when 30 real minutes have genuinely elapsed. (2) route.ts's debounce comparison now reads `Date.now()` instead of `Date.parse(openedAt)` — defense in depth: the debounce clock should never derive from client-supplied input at all, bounded or not, so this removes the dependency entirely rather than relying solely on the skew bound holding. Deliberately kept both changes scoped to this one route/helper pair; no other consumer of lastPreviewOpenAt (quality-score.ts, outreach-followups.ts) needed changes since both already just trust whatever resolvePreviewOpenedAt returns.\n\nUpdated preview-open.test.ts: replaced the now-inaccurate \"accepts a plausible past timestamp unchanged\" case (its own 2020-vs-2026 fixture was itself only plausible under the pre-fix, unbounded-past contract) with three cases mirroring the future-skew tests exactly — implausibly old (2001) falls back, just-past-tolerance falls back, just-within-tolerance is accepted unchanged — and fixed two pre-existing, otherwise-unrelated cases (\"accepts exact-max length when parseable\", \"normalizes parseable non-ISO dates to toISOString\") whose fixture dates sat ~4 days before their serverNow constant and so were newly (correctly) clamped by the past bound; moved both fixtures to sit inside the tolerance window without changing what each test actually asserts. Added one route.test.ts case asserting a spoofed 2001 openedAt gets clamped to within 60 seconds of real Date.now() before being handed to recordLeadPreviewOpen, proving the persisted checkpoint stays honest.\n\nVerified: npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json (0 errors) + npm run lint (clean) + npx vitest run src/lib/leads/preview-open.test.ts src/app/api/preview/[slug]/open/route.test.ts (38 passed) + npm test (2361 passed, 176 files = 2358 baseline + 3 new) + npm run build (clean, same 34-route ○/ƒ split). Reproduced the exact pre-fix exploit against the shipped fix with a real npm run dev pass on a fresh local.db (no NEXT_PUBLIC_CONVEX_URL configured in this sandbox, so SQLite fallback is the live path): POSTed the same 2001 spoof to /api/preview/serene-salon/open and confirmed via node:sqlite that lastPreviewOpenAt was written as real server time, not the spoofed date; confirmed an immediate real follow-up call correctly debounced (recorded:false, reason:\"debounced\" — legitimate debounce behavior unaffected); confirmed a second attacker POST with a different spoofed value (\"2001-01-01T00:31:00.000Z\", claiming 31 more spoofed minutes had passed) still correctly debounced, proving the repeated-call bypass is closed, not just the single-call case; confirmed /, /dashboard, /dashboard/leads, /preview/serene-salon, /docs/process all returned 200 throughout. Reset the test lead's lastPreviewOpenAt to NULL and confirmed zero other row changes, then stopped the dev server and confirmed port 3100 free; deleted local.db afterward. NEXT_STEPS: unchanged (all five gated items stayed as-is) — this is a security/correctness bug fix on already-shipped code, not a new backlog item.",
