    id: "sqlite-db-injectable-query-extraction",
    decision:
      "src/lib/db.ts (the entire Convex/SQLite persistence layer, ~3,660 lines) had zero direct automated test coverage, and structurally could not get any: it starts with `import \"server-only\"`, which throws unconditionally when imported outside a react-server runtime condition, so any Vitest file that imports it (even transitively) crashes immediately — confirmed by grepping every one of the 39 test files that reference `@/lib/db`: all 39 use `vi.mock(\"@/lib/db\", ...)`, zero import it for real. This means none of the 30+ raw SQL statements in the SQLite fallback path — including the newest-first ORDER BY that must stay in sync with the Convex twin (the sort-order-parity invariant AGENT_PROTOCOL's dual-backend rules repeatedly warn about) and upsertLead's check-then-insert create-only race guard — ever executed under CI. AGENT_PROTOCOL already names the fix pattern (src/lib/commerce/stripe-sync.ts's injectable-client shape: take the real client as a parameter so the logic itself needs no server-only guard) but it had never been applied to db.ts itself, only to smaller already-extracted row-mapping modules (leads/orders/deploy/checkout/inquiries/voice `sqlite-row-mapping.ts` files, which do have tests). Extracted the two highest-invariant surfaces as a first application of that pattern to db.ts: (1) src/lib/db/sqlite-schema.ts — initializeSqliteSchema(db: DatabaseSync), a pure code-move of the full CREATE TABLE / additive-column migration / demo-lead-seed block out of getSqliteDb(), no behavior change; (2) src/lib/db/sqlite-queries.ts — sqliteListOrders, sqliteListOrdersByLeadSlug, and sqliteUpsertLead, each taking `db: DatabaseSync` as a parameter, again pure code-moves of the exact SQL/logic previously inlined in db.ts's listOrders/listOrdersByLeadSlug/upsertLead. db.ts's getSqliteDb() and those three exported functions now delegate to the extracted modules instead of inlining the SQL — same behavior, now unit-testable.",
    rationale:
      "Found by a research agent explicitly briefed on every already-covered AGENT_PROTOCOL audit category (money/ledger integrity, webhook idempotency, Convex/SQLite parity, XSS/injection, rate limiting, redirect validation, field-size bounds, CSP, dead code, npm audit/outdated, branch fold-scanning, sitemap revalidation, catalog copy accuracy) and asked to find genuinely fresh, real ground rather than repeat any of them — every prior audit read db.ts's *callers* for correctness but none questioned whether db.ts's own SQLite fallback path had ever actually executed under test. This is real, not speculative: `git log --oneline -10 -- src/lib/db.ts` shows the last 9 commits touching it are all correctness fixes (atomic create-only race guards, paid→live stage-regression guards, duplicate-send guards) that shipped with only manual `npm run dev` spot-checks as verification per AGENT_PROTOCOL's own testing rule, never a regression test — meaning a future refactor could silently re-break any of them (a dropped WHERE, a flipped ORDER BY direction, a removed existence check) and every layer of this repo's CI (tsc/lint/the full Vitest suite) would stay green. Added src/lib/db/sqlite-schema.test.ts (schema creates every expected table without throwing, seeds demo leads once, is idempotent against a second initialize call on the same db, adds every migration column this app depends on, and enforces the orders.stripeSessionId unique index that backs Stripe webhook-redelivery idempotency) and src/lib/db/sqlite-queries.test.ts (sqliteUpsertLead: insert-then-return-true, second call on the same slug returns false without duplicating or overwriting the first row's data, optional fields persist correctly; sqliteListOrders/sqliteListOrdersByLeadSlug: newest-first ordering across three out-of-order-inserted rows, per-slug scoping keeps two leads' orders separate with the same sort contract, empty-orders case) — all against a real in-memory node:sqlite DatabaseSync(':memory:'), no mocking. This is a first application, not the full extraction: db.ts still has many other inline SQL statements (deploy handoffs, client inquiries, checkout sessions, marketing/voice packs, commerce catalogs/orders) with the same untested-server-only problem — a future iteration can repeat this exact pattern (one or two functions at a time, pure code-move, add a `:memory:`-backed test) rather than attempting a single large risky rewrite of the whole file. Verified: 11 new Vitest cases (5 schema + 6 query, all passing against real in-memory SQLite) + npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json (0 errors) + npm run lint (clean) + npm test (2456 passed = 2445 baseline + 11 new, 187 files) + a full clean rebuild (`rm -rf .next && npm run build`, clean, same 36-route ○/ƒ split) + npm run dev sanity check (deleted local.db, curled /dashboard/leads, /preview/example-bakery, /sitemap.xml — all 200, confirmed 'Seeded SQLite database with initial demo leads.' in the dev log with no errors, killed the dev server, deleted local.db afterward). This iteration's baseline checks (fresh npm ci, diff-gate against b0caef2 showing only the three already-reviewed product commits since, a ~25-branch fold-scan finding zero live candidates, PR #202 still the same stale draft, GitHub Actions runner outage reconfirmed unresolved at 30/30 sampled conclusion=failure, no live Stripe/Resend/Convex/Anthropic secret in this session's environment) all otherwise came back clean/unchanged and are not separately logged as their own entry. NEXT_STEPS unchanged (all five gated items stayed as-is) — this is a test-coverage/tech-debt fix, not a resolution of any gated item.",
